.lua
book.chapter(18, "Functors and reusable architecture")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Parameterize a family of related operations"
.pp
A functor accepts a module matching a signature and produces a module.
It is useful when an algorithm depends on a type together with several
operations on that type. Passing one callback is often enough for a local
sort, but a reusable package of ordering, lookup, and rendering operations
may need all clients to agree on the same abstract element type. A functor
makes that collection of dependencies explicit.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[module type SHOWABLE := sig
  type t
  val show : t -> String
end
module Show_int : SHOWABLE := struct
  type t = Int
  val show := Int.to_string
end
module Label (X : SHOWABLE) := struct
  val render x := "value=" ^ X.show x
end
module Int_label := Label(Show_int)
Test.equal "module parameter" (Int_label.render 42) "value=42"]=],
    "A rendering functor")
.endlua
.pp
The parameter X is a module, not an ordinary record value. The body can use
the operations admitted by SHOWABLE and the associated type X.t. It should
not rely on extra implementation details of Show_int. The application
Label(Show_int) occurs in module syntax and is bound to a module name before
its values are used. It is not a value-level function suitable for List.map.
.sh 2 "Associated types keep dependencies coherent"
.pp
Suppose a collection implementation needs equality and rendering for one
element type. Two unrelated callbacks could accidentally be configured with
different expectations unless their value-level types happen to express the
relationship clearly. A module signature can group type t, equal, and show,
so the relationship is part of one argument contract. The functor's returned
operations then share that same type consistently.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[module type ELEMENT := sig
  type t
  val equal : t -> t -> Bool
  val show : t -> String
end
module Integer_element : ELEMENT := struct
  type t = Int
  val equal a b := a == b
  val show := Int.to_string
end
module Collection (E : ELEMENT) := struct
  val contains item xs := List.any (fn x -> E.equal item x) xs
  val render xs := xs |> List.map E.show |> String.join ","
end
module Integers := Collection(Integer_element)
Test.check "membership" (Integers.contains 2 [1, 2, 3])
Test.equal "shared element interface" (Integers.render [1, 2, 3]) "1,2,3"]=],
    "A module argument couples a type with its operations")
.endlua
.pp
This example remains simple enough that higher-order functions would also
work. Its purpose is to expose the architectural boundary. In a larger tool,
an element module might also supply hashing, stable ordering, source
rendering, and validation. A signature can require those operations to agree
on one type while the functor builds several related data-processing
functions around them.
.sh 2 "Choose the smallest useful abstraction"
.pp
Use a higher-order function when one operation and its input types are all
the algorithm needs. List.sort_by is clearer than a one-use module functor
whose sole purpose is to pass a comparator. Use a functor when the returned
module exposes a coherent family of operations or owns types related to the
argument module. The distinction is about dependency structure, not about
which mechanism appears more sophisticated.
.lua
book.uml({
    {id="E", name="ELEMENT", members={"type t", "equal; show"}},
    {id="F", name="Collection(E)", members={"contains", "render"}},
    {id="C", name="Client", members={"uses one element identity"}}
}, {{"E", "F", "parameter"}, {"F", "C", "exports"}},
    "Functor dependencies are part of the module interface")
.endlua
.pp
A result signature can restrict what the functor returns. This is useful
when the implementation contains helper modules or representation types that
clients should not observe. Apply the same abstraction discipline as for an
ordinary structure: expose the relationships clients need and hide accidental
details. A functor is not an excuse to export an entire implementation scope
merely because it is generated from a parameter.
.sh 2 "Type identity is not representation equality"
.pp
Opaque abstract types retain public identities. Two modules may both use Int
internally without promising that their public types are interchangeable.
Fresh module applications and sealing therefore require care when sharing
values between instances. If an equality is part of the API, expose a
manifest alias or an explicit conversion. Do not infer it from the source
representation you happened to see while debugging.
.pp
For compiler architecture, this distinction can be beneficial. A parsed-name
type and a resolved-symbol type may both be represented by strings or
integers, yet mixing them would be a bug. Separate identities make the stage
boundary visible. A conversion operation can carry validation or lookup,
while an accidental raw cast would bypass that work. Types become a way to
express the pipeline's trust transitions.
.sh 2 "Initialization still has a runtime story"
.pp
Functor application does not make module bodies pure or eager. A result can
capture its argument module and initialize lazily when forced. Effects in
either the argument or result module can therefore occur at first use rather
than at the apparent textual application site. Keep initialization mostly
pure and make effectful startup explicit if clients need predictable timing.
Parameterization and initialization are orthogonal design questions.
.pp
Test a functor with more than one implementation of its parameter signature.
This exposes hidden assumptions that a single concrete implementation can
mask. For example, use a renderer with a visibly different format or an
equality relation that deliberately ignores an annotation. Then verify the
returned operations use the supplied contract rather than a hard-coded
comparison or display routine. Such tests validate the abstraction rather
than only its first instantiation.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Inspect functor parsing, module-argument checking, and runtime application in
both MetaML implementations. Extend the Collection example with a second
element module for strings, then compare it with passing callbacks directly
to standalone functions. Design a signature for a symbol service containing
an abstract symbol type, interning, and rendering. Explain which returned
operations must share that symbol identity and therefore benefit from being
constructed together by one functor application.
